1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,977
Start the engine of a normal helicopter,

2
00:00:02,977 --> 00:00:05,284
take away one small part at the

3
00:00:05,284 --> 00:00:08,113
back, and the whole machine would spin

4
00:00:08,113 --> 00:00:10,867
around in circles the moment it tried

5
00:00:10,867 --> 00:00:11,760
to lift off.

6
00:00:11,940 --> 00:00:14,472
That small part is the tail rotor,

7
00:00:14,472 --> 00:00:16,631
and it is only the beginning.

8
00:00:16,811 --> 00:00:20,175
Helicopters are full of parts that look

9
00:00:20,175 --> 00:00:22,246
strange from the ground,

10
00:00:22,246 --> 00:00:24,144
a fan inside the tail,

11
00:00:24,144 --> 00:00:26,042
a nut with a nickname,

12
00:00:26,042 --> 00:00:28,026
blades that cut cables.

13
00:00:28,206 --> 00:00:31,856
Today, ten parts that keep a helicopter

14
00:00:31,856 --> 00:00:35,039
from spinning, shaking or falling,

15
00:00:35,039 --> 00:00:37,285
and why each one exists.

16
00:00:37,635 --> 00:00:40,180
A helicopter is one of the hardest

17
00:00:40,180 --> 00:00:41,977
machines to keep stable.

18
00:00:42,157 --> 00:00:44,139
Its wing is a set of blades

19
00:00:44,139 --> 00:00:46,709
turning hundreds of times a minute,

20
00:00:46,709 --> 00:00:48,985
its engine fights its own body,

21
00:00:48,985 --> 00:00:50,453
and it has to hover,

22
00:00:50,453 --> 00:00:52,803
climb, and land almost anywhere.

23
00:00:52,983 --> 00:00:55,293
Every part we will look at today

24
00:00:55,293 --> 00:00:57,386
solves one of those problems,

25
00:00:57,386 --> 00:00:59,841
and some of them were written into

26
00:00:59,841 --> 00:01:01,934
the design after people died.

27
00:01:02,285 --> 00:01:05,069
As always, every fact comes from a

28
00:01:05,069 --> 00:01:07,771
named source, aviation handbooks,

29
00:01:07,771 --> 00:01:10,637
manufacturers and accident reports,

30
00:01:10,637 --> 00:01:13,093
all listed in the description.

31
00:01:13,273 --> 00:01:15,717
Let's start with the problem that every

32
00:01:15,717 --> 00:01:17,221
helicopter is born with.

33
00:01:17,571 --> 00:01:19,859
Why is there a small rotor on

34
00:01:19,859 --> 00:01:21,831
the tail of a helicopter?

35
00:01:22,011 --> 00:01:24,217
Because of Newton's third law.

36
00:01:24,397 --> 00:01:26,764
When the engine pushes the big rotor

37
00:01:26,764 --> 00:01:28,341
around in one direction,

38
00:01:28,341 --> 00:01:30,445
the rotor pushes the body of the

39
00:01:30,445 --> 00:01:33,140
helicopter around in the other direction.

40
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:35,325
Without something to stop it,

41
00:01:35,325 --> 00:01:37,952
the cabin would spin under the blades.

42
00:01:38,132 --> 00:01:41,524
The tail rotor pushes sideways against that

43
00:01:41,524 --> 00:01:44,207
spin, like a hand holding the tail

44
00:01:44,207 --> 00:01:44,680
still.

45
00:01:45,030 --> 00:01:47,853
This layout, one main rotor and one

46
00:01:47,853 --> 00:01:49,789
small rotor at the tail,

47
00:01:49,789 --> 00:01:52,934
was perfected by Igor Sikorsky with his

48
00:01:52,934 --> 00:01:56,322
VS-300 in the years around nineteen forty,

49
00:01:56,322 --> 00:01:58,823
and it is still the most common

50
00:01:58,823 --> 00:02:00,436
design in the world.

51
00:02:00,616 --> 00:02:01,765
It is not free.

52
00:02:01,945 --> 00:02:04,689
About ten percent of the engine power

53
00:02:04,689 --> 00:02:06,394
goes to the tail rotor,

54
00:02:06,394 --> 00:02:08,990
power that does nothing to lift the

55
00:02:08,990 --> 00:02:09,805
helicopter.

56
00:02:09,985 --> 00:02:12,629
The pilot controls it with two pedals

57
00:02:12,629 --> 00:02:14,987
that change the pitch of the tail

58
00:02:14,987 --> 00:02:17,559
blades, and that is how a helicopter

59
00:02:17,559 --> 00:02:19,703
turns its nose while hovering.

60
00:02:20,053 --> 00:02:23,004
The tail rotor spins so fast that

61
00:02:23,004 --> 00:02:24,882
it almost disappears.

62
00:02:25,062 --> 00:02:27,446
That is why tail rotor blades are

63
00:02:27,446 --> 00:02:29,469
painted with bright stripes,

64
00:02:29,469 --> 00:02:31,781
often red and white or black and

65
00:02:31,781 --> 00:02:32,214
white.

66
00:02:32,394 --> 00:02:34,853
A spinning tail rotor is a hazard

67
00:02:34,853 --> 00:02:38,132
to anyone walking near a running helicopter,

68
00:02:38,132 --> 00:02:40,964
and the stripes turn an invisible disc

69
00:02:40,964 --> 00:02:43,423
into something the eye can catch.

70
00:02:43,603 --> 00:02:45,121
On a helicopter,

71
00:02:45,121 --> 00:02:47,493
even the paint has a job.

72
00:02:47,843 --> 00:02:50,707
Why do some helicopters have their tail

73
00:02:50,707 --> 00:02:53,277
rotor hidden inside a round hole in

74
00:02:53,277 --> 00:02:53,938
the tail?

75
00:02:54,118 --> 00:02:56,440
It is called a Fenestron,

76
00:02:56,440 --> 00:02:58,297
a fan inside a duct.

77
00:02:58,477 --> 00:03:00,686
It first flew on the twelfth of

78
00:03:00,686 --> 00:03:02,610
April nineteen sixty-eight,

79
00:03:02,610 --> 00:03:05,246
on a prototype of the French Gazelle.

80
00:03:05,426 --> 00:03:08,552
The engineers' first goal was protection,

81
00:03:08,552 --> 00:03:11,221
of the people working on the ground

82
00:03:11,221 --> 00:03:13,432
and of the tail rotor itself,

83
00:03:13,432 --> 00:03:15,186
which can hit branches,

84
00:03:15,186 --> 00:03:16,711
wires or the ground.

85
00:03:17,061 --> 00:03:19,076
The duct around the fan does more

86
00:03:19,076 --> 00:03:19,870
than protect.

87
00:03:20,050 --> 00:03:22,750
It makes the tail rotor quieter,

88
00:03:22,750 --> 00:03:25,786
and on newer versions the blades are

89
00:03:25,786 --> 00:03:28,064
spaced unevenly on purpose,

90
00:03:28,064 --> 00:03:31,270
so the sound spreads across many tones

91
00:03:31,270 --> 00:03:33,378
instead of one loud buzz.

92
00:03:33,558 --> 00:03:37,700
Airbus introduced that uneven spacing on the

93
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:40,524
H135 in the nineteen nineties.

94
00:03:40,704 --> 00:03:41,871
On the H160,

95
00:03:41,871 --> 00:03:45,662
the whole fan is tilted twelve degrees,

96
00:03:45,662 --> 00:03:48,773
so that it also helps with lift.

97
00:03:49,124 --> 00:03:54,184
Today the Fenestron is on many Airbus

98
00:03:54,184 --> 00:03:57,877
helicopters, like the H130,

99
00:03:57,877 --> 00:04:02,254
the H135, the H145 and the H160.

100
00:04:02,434 --> 00:04:04,765
It is heavier than an open tail

101
00:04:04,765 --> 00:04:07,772
rotor, which is why not every helicopter

102
00:04:07,772 --> 00:04:08,448
uses one.

103
00:04:08,628 --> 00:04:11,634
But when a helicopter lands next to

104
00:04:11,634 --> 00:04:14,467
people, in a hospital car park or

105
00:04:14,467 --> 00:04:15,755
a city rooftop,

106
00:04:15,755 --> 00:04:17,816
a fan inside a hole is a

107
00:04:17,816 --> 00:04:20,993
lot safer than blades spinning in the

108
00:04:20,993 --> 00:04:21,423
open.

109
00:04:21,773 --> 00:04:24,383
Why do some helicopters have no tail

110
00:04:24,383 --> 00:04:25,325
rotor at all?

111
00:04:25,505 --> 00:04:26,656
This is NOTAR,

112
00:04:26,656 --> 00:04:28,628
short for no tail rotor.

113
00:04:28,808 --> 00:04:30,582
Inside the tail boom,

114
00:04:30,582 --> 00:04:33,286
a fan blows low pressure air out

115
00:04:33,286 --> 00:04:35,821
of two long slots on the side.

116
00:04:36,001 --> 00:04:38,495
The air coming down from the main

117
00:04:38,495 --> 00:04:40,537
rotor hugs the curved boom,

118
00:04:40,537 --> 00:04:43,183
and that bends the flow and creates

119
00:04:43,183 --> 00:04:44,468
a sideways force,

120
00:04:44,468 --> 00:04:45,753
just like a wing.

121
00:04:45,933 --> 00:04:49,579
The tail boom itself becomes the anti-torque

122
00:04:49,579 --> 00:04:50,159
device.

123
00:04:50,509 --> 00:04:52,730
At the very end of the tail,

124
00:04:52,730 --> 00:04:55,666
a rotating nozzle called a direct jet

125
00:04:55,666 --> 00:04:57,967
thruster pushes out more air,

126
00:04:57,967 --> 00:05:00,427
and the pilot uses it to steer,

127
00:05:00,427 --> 00:05:03,045
with the same pedals as any other

128
00:05:03,045 --> 00:05:03,918
helicopter.

129
00:05:04,098 --> 00:05:06,205
A NOTAR test aircraft,

130
00:05:06,205 --> 00:05:07,834
a modified OH-6A,

131
00:05:07,834 --> 00:05:11,761
first flew on the seventeenth of December

132
00:05:11,761 --> 00:05:13,676
nineteen eighty-one.

133
00:05:13,856 --> 00:05:17,550
The production MD five twenty N followed

134
00:05:17,550 --> 00:05:19,304
in nineteen ninety,

135
00:05:19,304 --> 00:05:21,797
built by McDonnell Douglas,

136
00:05:21,797 --> 00:05:23,736
later MD Helicopters.

137
00:05:24,087 --> 00:05:26,908
The reasons are safety and noise.

138
00:05:27,088 --> 00:05:29,812
With no blades spinning at the back,

139
00:05:29,812 --> 00:05:32,536
nobody can walk into the tail rotor,

140
00:05:32,536 --> 00:05:34,806
and the tail cannot hit a tree

141
00:05:34,806 --> 00:05:35,563
or a wire.

142
00:05:35,743 --> 00:05:38,332
Tail rotors also make a large part

143
00:05:38,332 --> 00:05:40,160
of a helicopter's noise,

144
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:43,207
which is why NOTAR helicopters are among

145
00:05:43,207 --> 00:05:46,710
the quietest certified by the Federal Aviation

146
00:05:46,710 --> 00:05:47,852
Administration.

147
00:05:48,032 --> 00:05:50,238
That is why you often see them

148
00:05:50,238 --> 00:05:53,032
with police and news crews flying over

149
00:05:53,032 --> 00:05:53,547
cities.

150
00:05:53,897 --> 00:05:56,540
Why do some helicopters have two rotors

151
00:05:56,540 --> 00:05:58,506
stacked on top of each other?

152
00:05:58,686 --> 00:06:01,600
Because if two rotors turn in opposite

153
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:04,437
directions, their torque cancels out,

154
00:06:04,437 --> 00:06:07,428
and the helicopter needs no tail rotor.

155
00:06:07,608 --> 00:06:10,608
This is called a coaxial design,

156
00:06:10,608 --> 00:06:14,078
and the Russian company Kamov made it

157
00:06:14,078 --> 00:06:18,203
its trademark, on helicopters like the Ka-27

158
00:06:18,203 --> 00:06:21,203
and the Ka-52 attack helicopter.

159
00:06:21,554 --> 00:06:22,924
There is a bonus.

160
00:06:23,104 --> 00:06:25,764
Every bit of engine power goes into

161
00:06:25,764 --> 00:06:28,500
lift instead of ten percent going to

162
00:06:28,500 --> 00:06:29,489
a tail rotor,

163
00:06:29,489 --> 00:06:32,073
and the helicopter can be shorter,

164
00:06:32,073 --> 00:06:34,657
which matters on the small deck of

165
00:06:34,657 --> 00:06:35,190
a ship.

166
00:06:35,370 --> 00:06:39,363
Coaxial helicopters also handle crosswinds well,

167
00:06:39,363 --> 00:06:42,109
because there is no tail rotor to

168
00:06:42,109 --> 00:06:43,357
fight the wind.

169
00:06:43,537 --> 00:06:46,250
The price is a tall and complex

170
00:06:46,250 --> 00:06:49,314
rotor head, with two sets of blades

171
00:06:49,314 --> 00:06:51,502
and controls on one mast.

172
00:06:51,852 --> 00:06:54,789
The two rotors must also never touch.

173
00:06:54,969 --> 00:06:56,634
When a blade flexes,

174
00:06:56,634 --> 00:06:59,298
it bends toward the other rotor,

175
00:06:59,298 --> 00:07:02,379
so coaxial designs keep a careful gap

176
00:07:02,379 --> 00:07:04,210
between the two discs.

177
00:07:04,390 --> 00:07:06,666
The same idea is now used on

178
00:07:06,666 --> 00:07:09,917
new high speed designs like the Sikorsky

179
00:07:09,917 --> 00:07:12,761
X2 family, where stiff rotors and a

180
00:07:12,761 --> 00:07:16,175
pusher propeller let a helicopter fly much

181
00:07:16,175 --> 00:07:18,206
faster than a normal one.

182
00:07:18,557 --> 00:07:20,677
Why does the Chinook have a rotor

183
00:07:20,677 --> 00:07:21,447
at each end?

184
00:07:21,627 --> 00:07:24,066
Same trick, different place.

185
00:07:24,246 --> 00:07:27,456
The two rotors turn in opposite directions,

186
00:07:27,456 --> 00:07:30,143
so their torque cancels and there is

187
00:07:30,143 --> 00:07:31,188
no tail rotor.

188
00:07:31,368 --> 00:07:34,678
The Chinook first flew in September nineteen

189
00:07:34,678 --> 00:07:37,837
sixty-one, and more than six decades later

190
00:07:37,837 --> 00:07:39,868
it is still one of the best

191
00:07:39,868 --> 00:07:42,200
known helicopters in the world.

192
00:07:42,551 --> 00:07:45,476
Two big rotors mean the Chinook can

193
00:07:45,476 --> 00:07:46,896
lift heavy loads,

194
00:07:46,896 --> 00:07:49,821
and because all the power goes into

195
00:07:49,821 --> 00:07:52,579
lift, nothing is wasted on a tail

196
00:07:52,579 --> 00:07:53,081
rotor.

197
00:07:53,261 --> 00:07:55,448
It also has a very long cabin

198
00:07:55,448 --> 00:07:57,861
that can be loaded from the back

199
00:07:57,861 --> 00:07:58,992
through a ramp,

200
00:07:58,992 --> 00:08:02,084
with vehicles, cargo or dozens of troops.

201
00:08:02,264 --> 00:08:05,109
The two rotors overlap in the middle,

202
00:08:05,109 --> 00:08:07,184
so a shaft along the top of

203
00:08:07,184 --> 00:08:10,182
the cabin keeps them perfectly in time.

204
00:08:10,362 --> 00:08:12,401
If they ever got out of step,

205
00:08:12,401 --> 00:08:14,159
the blades would collide.

206
00:08:14,509 --> 00:08:16,424
Steering is clever too.

207
00:08:16,604 --> 00:08:19,207
To turn, the pilot tilts the front

208
00:08:19,207 --> 00:08:21,657
rotor one way and the rear rotor

209
00:08:21,657 --> 00:08:22,728
the other way,

210
00:08:22,728 --> 00:08:26,020
so the helicopter swings around its middle.

211
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:27,314
To fly forward,

212
00:08:27,314 --> 00:08:29,764
the rotors tilt forward together.

213
00:08:29,944 --> 00:08:32,400
The front rotor sits lower than the

214
00:08:32,400 --> 00:08:34,645
rear one, so the rear rotor does

215
00:08:34,645 --> 00:08:37,311
not fly through the disturbed air from

216
00:08:37,311 --> 00:08:38,013
the front.

217
00:08:38,363 --> 00:08:40,815
Why is there a strange tilting ring

218
00:08:40,815 --> 00:08:42,287
under the main rotor?

219
00:08:42,467 --> 00:08:44,545
It is called the swashplate,

220
00:08:44,545 --> 00:08:46,698
and it is how the pilot talks

221
00:08:46,698 --> 00:08:47,738
to the blades.

222
00:08:47,918 --> 00:08:49,485
It has two parts.

223
00:08:49,665 --> 00:08:52,069
The bottom plate does not spin and

224
00:08:52,069 --> 00:08:54,402
is moved by the pilot's controls.

225
00:08:54,582 --> 00:08:57,094
The top plate spins with the rotor

226
00:08:57,094 --> 00:08:59,681
and is connected to each blade with

227
00:08:59,681 --> 00:09:00,050
rods.

228
00:09:00,230 --> 00:09:02,192
When the bottom plate moves,

229
00:09:02,192 --> 00:09:03,734
the top plate follows,

230
00:09:03,734 --> 00:09:06,116
and the blades change their angle.

231
00:09:06,467 --> 00:09:08,045
The collective lever,

232
00:09:08,045 --> 00:09:09,924
at the pilot's left hand,

233
00:09:09,924 --> 00:09:12,028
raises the whole swashplate.

234
00:09:12,208 --> 00:09:14,727
Every blade takes a bigger bite of

235
00:09:14,727 --> 00:09:16,283
air at the same time,

236
00:09:16,283 --> 00:09:18,210
and the helicopter climbs.

237
00:09:18,390 --> 00:09:19,818
The cyclic stick,

238
00:09:19,818 --> 00:09:22,002
between the pilot's knees,

239
00:09:22,002 --> 00:09:23,766
tilts the swashplate.

240
00:09:23,946 --> 00:09:26,749
Now each blade changes its angle as

241
00:09:26,749 --> 00:09:27,950
it goes around,

242
00:09:27,950 --> 00:09:29,712
more bite on one side,

243
00:09:29,712 --> 00:09:31,154
less on the other,

244
00:09:31,154 --> 00:09:33,797
and the whole rotor disc tilts in

245
00:09:33,797 --> 00:09:34,998
that direction.

246
00:09:35,349 --> 00:09:37,947
A helicopter does not fly in a

247
00:09:37,947 --> 00:09:40,631
direction by pointing its nose.

248
00:09:40,811 --> 00:09:42,402
It tilts its rotor.

249
00:09:42,582 --> 00:09:45,028
Tilt the disc forward and part of

250
00:09:45,028 --> 00:09:46,808
the lift pushes forward.

251
00:09:46,988 --> 00:09:50,168
Tilt it left and the helicopter slides

252
00:09:50,168 --> 00:09:50,587
left.

253
00:09:50,767 --> 00:09:53,854
All of that happens through one tilting

254
00:09:53,854 --> 00:09:55,200
ring, a few rods,

255
00:09:55,200 --> 00:09:58,050
and blades that twist a little every

256
00:09:58,050 --> 00:10:00,821
single turn, many times per second.

257
00:10:01,171 --> 00:10:03,029
Why is the nut at the very

258
00:10:03,029 --> 00:10:05,889
top of some helicopter rotors called the

259
00:10:05,889 --> 00:10:06,604
Jesus nut?

260
00:10:06,784 --> 00:10:09,264
It is the main rotor retaining nut,

261
00:10:09,264 --> 00:10:11,673
the part that holds the rotor onto

262
00:10:11,673 --> 00:10:12,310
the mast.

263
00:10:12,490 --> 00:10:14,778
If it ever came off in flight,

264
00:10:14,778 --> 00:10:17,599
the rotor would leave the helicopter,

265
00:10:17,599 --> 00:10:20,344
and the crew would have nothing left

266
00:10:20,344 --> 00:10:21,488
to do but pray.

267
00:10:21,668 --> 00:10:24,983
The nickname appeared in print in nineteen

268
00:10:24,983 --> 00:10:28,693
sixty-seven, and it became famous through books

269
00:10:28,693 --> 00:10:31,455
about helicopter pilots in Vietnam.

270
00:10:31,806 --> 00:10:33,458
Real failures are rare,

271
00:10:33,458 --> 00:10:35,973
because the nut and its locking pin

272
00:10:35,973 --> 00:10:38,272
are checked before every flight.

273
00:10:38,452 --> 00:10:39,648
But it can happen.

274
00:10:39,828 --> 00:10:41,152
In two thousand,

275
00:10:41,152 --> 00:10:43,717
a Bell two oh six crashed about

276
00:10:43,717 --> 00:10:45,869
ten minutes after takeoff,

277
00:10:45,869 --> 00:10:48,269
killing both people on board.

278
00:10:48,449 --> 00:10:50,649
The mast nut had been removed for

279
00:10:50,649 --> 00:10:53,317
repainting and had not been properly put

280
00:10:53,317 --> 00:10:54,451
back and checked.

281
00:10:54,801 --> 00:10:57,844
Today engineers use Jesus nut to describe

282
00:10:57,844 --> 00:11:00,888
any single part whose failure would bring

283
00:11:00,888 --> 00:11:02,521
down the whole system.

284
00:11:02,701 --> 00:11:06,011
Most of aviation is designed to avoid

285
00:11:06,011 --> 00:11:08,248
single points of failure,

286
00:11:08,248 --> 00:11:10,306
with two of everything.

287
00:11:10,486 --> 00:11:13,214
A helicopter rotor is one of the

288
00:11:13,214 --> 00:11:16,283
few places where one part simply has

289
00:11:16,283 --> 00:11:18,414
to hold, so it is made to

290
00:11:18,414 --> 00:11:21,909
be strong, simple and inspected again and

291
00:11:21,909 --> 00:11:22,421
again.

292
00:11:22,771 --> 00:11:25,762
Why are there small shock absorbers at

293
00:11:25,762 --> 00:11:28,123
the root of helicopter blades?

294
00:11:28,303 --> 00:11:31,345
They prevent something called ground resonance.

295
00:11:31,525 --> 00:11:32,587
On many rotors,

296
00:11:32,587 --> 00:11:34,923
the blades are hinged so they can

297
00:11:34,923 --> 00:11:37,260
move a little forward and back as

298
00:11:37,260 --> 00:11:37,968
they turn.

299
00:11:38,148 --> 00:11:40,804
If those blade movements fall out of

300
00:11:40,804 --> 00:11:43,238
step and match the rocking of the

301
00:11:43,238 --> 00:11:45,525
helicopter on its landing gear,

302
00:11:45,525 --> 00:11:48,771
the vibration can grow violently in seconds.

303
00:11:49,122 --> 00:11:50,518
In the worst cases,

304
00:11:50,518 --> 00:11:53,532
a helicopter sitting on the ground starts

305
00:11:53,532 --> 00:11:55,002
to shake, then rock,

306
00:11:55,002 --> 00:11:58,163
and then literally tears itself apart while

307
00:11:58,163 --> 00:12:00,221
the engine is still running.

308
00:12:00,401 --> 00:12:02,003
That is ground resonance.

309
00:12:02,183 --> 00:12:05,123
It was first seen on early gyroplanes

310
00:12:05,123 --> 00:12:08,619
and became a serious problem for helicopters

311
00:12:08,619 --> 00:12:10,844
during the Second World War.

312
00:12:11,024 --> 00:12:12,703
The cure is damping,

313
00:12:12,703 --> 00:12:14,886
lag dampers on the blades,

314
00:12:14,886 --> 00:12:18,665
either hydraulic or made of elastic material,

315
00:12:18,665 --> 00:12:21,519
plus carefully tuned landing gear.

316
00:12:21,870 --> 00:12:26,432
Military helicopters must pass ground resonance tests

317
00:12:26,432 --> 00:12:28,842
on slopes, with a flat tire,

318
00:12:28,842 --> 00:12:31,425
and even with a failed damper.

319
00:12:31,605 --> 00:12:33,996
And pilots learn the escape.

320
00:12:34,176 --> 00:12:35,276
If it starts,

321
00:12:35,276 --> 00:12:37,983
the best option is often to lift

322
00:12:37,983 --> 00:12:38,321
off.

323
00:12:38,501 --> 00:12:41,416
Once the helicopter is no longer touching

324
00:12:41,416 --> 00:12:44,330
the ground, the rocking stops feeding the

325
00:12:44,330 --> 00:12:47,174
vibration, and the machine settles down.

326
00:12:47,524 --> 00:12:50,168
Why do some helicopters have sharp blades

327
00:12:50,168 --> 00:12:52,361
sticking up from the roof and down

328
00:12:52,361 --> 00:12:53,329
from the belly?

329
00:12:53,509 --> 00:12:55,161
They are wire cutters,

330
00:12:55,161 --> 00:12:58,164
part of a wire strike protection system.

331
00:12:58,344 --> 00:13:00,086
Helicopters fly low,

332
00:13:00,086 --> 00:13:03,307
and power lines and cables are almost

333
00:13:03,307 --> 00:13:05,659
invisible from the cockpit.

334
00:13:05,839 --> 00:13:08,346
If the helicopter flies into a wire,

335
00:13:08,346 --> 00:13:10,784
it slides up or down the windshield

336
00:13:10,784 --> 00:13:11,969
into the cutters,

337
00:13:11,969 --> 00:13:13,710
and the cutters slice it.

338
00:13:14,060 --> 00:13:16,906
The system was developed in Canada by

339
00:13:16,906 --> 00:13:20,828
Bristol Aerospace, and in May nineteen seventy-nine

340
00:13:20,828 --> 00:13:23,443
it was qualified on the Bell Kiowa

341
00:13:23,443 --> 00:13:25,751
for the Canadian Armed Forces.

342
00:13:25,931 --> 00:13:28,160
It is designed to cut a steel

343
00:13:28,160 --> 00:13:31,004
cable three eighths of an inch thick.

344
00:13:31,184 --> 00:13:33,936
The United States Army tested it on

345
00:13:33,936 --> 00:13:36,608
the Kiowa, the Huey and the Cobra,

346
00:13:36,608 --> 00:13:38,966
and it was fitted to its small

347
00:13:38,966 --> 00:13:40,774
and medium helicopters.

348
00:13:41,125 --> 00:13:42,680
The reason was blood.

349
00:13:42,860 --> 00:13:47,659
Between nineteen seventy-four and nineteen seventy-nine,

350
00:13:47,659 --> 00:13:51,173
wire strikes caused sixteen percent of US

351
00:13:51,173 --> 00:13:53,658
Army aircraft related deaths.

352
00:13:53,838 --> 00:13:57,123
Today you see these cutters on military,

353
00:13:57,123 --> 00:14:00,079
rescue and agricultural helicopters,

354
00:14:00,079 --> 00:14:02,954
all the machines that work close to

355
00:14:02,954 --> 00:14:03,857
the ground.

356
00:14:04,037 --> 00:14:05,628
Two small knives,

357
00:14:05,628 --> 00:14:09,089
and one of the most invisible dangers

358
00:14:09,089 --> 00:14:11,990
in aviation becomes survivable.

359
00:14:12,340 --> 00:14:15,834
What happens if a helicopter's engine stops?

360
00:14:16,014 --> 00:14:18,046
It does not fall like a stone.

361
00:14:18,226 --> 00:14:20,544
Between the engine and the rotor,

362
00:14:20,544 --> 00:14:22,581
there is a freewheeling unit,

363
00:14:22,581 --> 00:14:25,390
a clutch that lets go automatically when

364
00:14:25,390 --> 00:14:28,129
the engine turns slower than the rotor.

365
00:14:28,309 --> 00:14:30,626
The rotor keeps spinning freely,

366
00:14:30,626 --> 00:14:32,870
and as the helicopter descends,

367
00:14:32,870 --> 00:14:35,693
air flowing up through the blades keeps

368
00:14:35,693 --> 00:14:36,634
them turning.

369
00:14:36,814 --> 00:14:38,821
It is called autorotation,

370
00:14:38,821 --> 00:14:41,446
the helicopter version of a glide.

371
00:14:41,796 --> 00:14:44,106
The pilot has to act fast.

372
00:14:44,286 --> 00:14:47,347
The collective must go down immediately,

373
00:14:47,347 --> 00:14:49,720
so the blades keep their speed.

374
00:14:49,900 --> 00:14:52,677
Then the helicopter glides down at a

375
00:14:52,677 --> 00:14:55,300
steep angle, and at about fifty to

376
00:14:55,300 --> 00:14:58,154
one hundred feet the pilot raises the

377
00:14:58,154 --> 00:15:00,468
nose and pulls the collective,

378
00:15:00,468 --> 00:15:03,477
using the energy stored in the spinning

379
00:15:03,477 --> 00:15:06,177
rotor to slow down and land softly.

380
00:15:06,357 --> 00:15:09,310
Every single engine helicopter must show it

381
00:15:09,310 --> 00:15:11,233
can do this to be certified.

382
00:15:11,583 --> 00:15:14,891
The most famous autorotation happened on the

383
00:15:14,891 --> 00:15:18,049
twenty-first of June nineteen seventy-two.

384
00:15:18,229 --> 00:15:21,171
Test pilot Jean Boulet flew a small

385
00:15:21,171 --> 00:15:24,953
Lama helicopter to about forty thousand eight

386
00:15:24,953 --> 00:15:27,390
hundred feet, a world record.

387
00:15:27,570 --> 00:15:30,119
Up there, in the extreme cold,

388
00:15:30,119 --> 00:15:33,094
the engine flamed out and could not

389
00:15:33,094 --> 00:15:34,199
be restarted.

390
00:15:34,379 --> 00:15:37,146
Boulet came all the way down with

391
00:15:37,146 --> 00:15:40,834
no engine, in the longest autorotation ever,

392
00:15:40,834 --> 00:15:42,343
and landed safely.

393
00:15:42,523 --> 00:15:45,287
His altitude record still stands.

394
00:15:45,637 --> 00:15:47,100
So here are the ten.

395
00:15:47,280 --> 00:15:50,445
A tail rotor that stops the spin,

396
00:15:50,445 --> 00:15:52,651
a fan hidden in a hole,

397
00:15:52,651 --> 00:15:55,337
a tail with no rotor at all.

398
00:15:55,517 --> 00:15:57,398
Two rotors on top,

399
00:15:57,398 --> 00:15:59,383
or one at each end.

400
00:15:59,563 --> 00:16:02,557
A tilting ring that steers the blades,

401
00:16:02,557 --> 00:16:04,685
a nut that must never fail,

402
00:16:04,685 --> 00:16:08,073
dampers that stop a helicopter from shaking

403
00:16:08,073 --> 00:16:11,304
itself apart, knives for invisible wires,

404
00:16:11,304 --> 00:16:13,825
and a clutch that lets the rotor

405
00:16:13,825 --> 00:16:16,583
keep spinning when the engine dies.

406
00:16:16,933 --> 00:16:19,722
A helicopter looks like it is fighting

407
00:16:19,722 --> 00:16:21,484
the air, and in a way it

408
00:16:21,484 --> 00:16:21,704
is.

409
00:16:21,884 --> 00:16:24,170
Every one of these parts is there

410
00:16:24,170 --> 00:16:26,524
because something went wrong once,

411
00:16:26,524 --> 00:16:27,771
or could go wrong.

412
00:16:27,951 --> 00:16:31,011
If you want more machines explained part

413
00:16:31,011 --> 00:16:33,918
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

414
00:16:34,098 --> 00:16:36,197
And tell us in the comments which

415
00:16:36,197 --> 00:16:38,296
machine you want us to take apart

416
00:16:38,296 --> 00:16:38,614
next.
